  "result": "Not quite. Overlapping is the goal, but sometimes an instruction has to wait, and that wait is called a stall.\n\nHere's a real case:\n\n```\nadd r1, r2, r3    # r1 = r2 + r3\nsub r4, r1, r5    # needs r1\n```\n\nThe `add` doesn't write r1 until near the end of its trip through the pipeline. The `sub` wants to read r1 early in its own trip. If they overlap fully, `sub` reads the old r1 and gets the wrong answer. So the pipeline holds `sub` back for a cycle or two, and those lost cycles are the stall cost.\n\nThat's one kind of wait. Pipelines can also stall when two instructions need the same hardware at the same moment, or when a branch makes the pipeline unsure which instruction comes next. That last one is why your branchy loop runs slower.\n\nWhy it matters for your CPU design question: more stages means more overlap, but also more cycles lost each time something has to wait.\n\nQuick try: in the example, which instruction has to wait, `add` or `sub`? Why?",
